md(n,'Each frame belongs to a **stream**, and a stream is one request/response exchange.','Frames address a **stream** or the connection (stream ID0). HTTP request/response exchanges use streams; connection-control frames are not individual requests.','Connection frames use streamzero, not application request stream.')
md(n,'A GET is one HEADERS frame with both END_HEADERS and END_STREAM set (flags `0x05`). The response is a HEADERS frame and one or more DATA frames, the last with END_STREAM.','A bodyless GET whose field block fits in one frame can use HEADERS with END_HEADERS and END_STREAM (0x05). Larger field blocks use CONTINUATION frames. A response may end on HEADERS, DATA or trailing HEADERS; informational responses and trailers add further possibilities.','Not all GETs/responseheaders fitoneframe or requireDATA.')
md(n,'Each starts with a window of **65,535 bytes**.','Before settings, each has a **65,535-byte** window. SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE adjusts stream windows; the connection window is enlarged with WINDOW_UPDATE.','SETTINGS cannot change connectionwindow.')
md(n,'Only DATA frames count.','Only DATA payloads count, including their pad-length and padding bytes, but excluding the9-byte frame header.','Clarify padding charged to flowcontrol.')
md(n,'But a small window caps throughput at window ÷ RTT: 65,535 bytes over a 100 ms RTT is about 5 Mbit/s per stream, which is why servers and browsers raise the initial window via SETTINGS.','In an ideal feedback model with prompt credit return, negligible transmission time and no other bottleneck, sustained throughput is approximately bounded by window/RTT: 65,535bytes over100ms gives5.24Mbit/s. Delayed credit and other bottlenecks lower it. The receiver can advertise more stream credit; connection credit must also suffice.','Throughput model needs creditroundtripassumptions and receiverdirection.')
md(n,'`GOAWAY` carries the highest stream ID the sender will process, so a client knows which requests to retry on a new connection.','GOAWAY reports the highest peer-initiated stream ID on which the sender might have acted or might still act. Higher-numbered requests were not processed and may be retried; lower-numbered unfinished requests may have ambiguous outcomes.','GOAWAY is processinguncertaintyboundary,not proofalllowerprocessed or safeanyretry.')
md(n,'Servers now limit how many resets a client may send.','Mitigations can limit abusive creation/reset work or terminate offending connections; deployed protection depends on implementation and configuration.','Do not claimuniversalpatchedservers.')
md(n,'The original tree-based priority scheme was complex and widely ignored, and RFC 9113 deprecated it.','RFC9113 deprecated the original tree-based priority scheme.','Removeunverifiedprevalence judgement.')
md(n,'**Server push** was rarely a win, often pushing resources the browser already had cached, and Chrome removed support in 2022.','Chrome’s published removal rationale included limited performance benefit and cache-related waste; it removed HTTP/2 server push support starting withChrome106 in2022. Other clients and non-browser use differ.','Attributevendorperformanceanalysis and versionscope.')
md(n,'def enc_int(value, n):\n    limit','def enc_int(value, n):\n    if (type(value) is not int or value < 0\n            or type(n) is not int\n            or not 1 <= n <= 8):\n        raise ValueError("bad integer")\n    limit','Rejectnegativevalue or invalidprefixwidth.')
md(n,'The next request to the same host sends `be` (`1` + 62) for that field: one byte instead of 15.','If the entry remains at index62 and the encoder chooses it, a later block can send be (1+62) for the whole field. The original literal field occupied17bytes (representation+length+15-byte value), not just15.','Dynamicindexcanmove/evict, exactwirecostwaswrongcomparison.')
md(n,'one lost TCP segment stalls them all.','a gap in TCP’s ordered byte stream blocks delivery of later bytes, potentially delaying otherwise independent HTTP/2 streams. Already delivered data remains usable.','Not everyloss stallsallstreams ifno laterbyteswaiting.')
md(n,'Each packet carries **frames**:','Protected data packets carry **frames** (Retry and Version Negotiation have different payload formats):','Retry/VNdo notcontainframes.')
md(n,'- **Long header** (first bit 1), used during the handshake. Types: Initial, 0-RTT, Handshake and Retry. It carries the version and both connection IDs, with their lengths.','- In QUICv1, **long headers** (first bit1) appear on Initial,0-RTT,Handshake andRetry packets; Version Negotiation has a related format. Version and connection IDs are visible, with ID lengths. These formats are used while establishing a connection; timing is not itself the discriminator.') if False else None
md(n,'- **Short header** (first bit 0), used for 1-RTT data after the handshake.','- **Short header** (first bit 0), used for1-RTT data once the appropriate keys are available, potentially before handshake confirmation.','1RTTpackets canprecedehandshakeconfirmation.')
md(n,'Almost all of this is encrypted. Even the packet number and low bits of the first byte are masked by **header protection**, so middleboxes cannot track or "help" with it. This deliberate opacity stops middleboxes from ossifying the protocol the way they did TCP.','The payload is encrypted. Header protection masks the packet number and selected low bits of the first byte (five for short headers, four for protected long headers). Connection IDs and other routing fields remain visible; the short-header spin bit is also outside that mask. This reduces what middleboxes can depend on, without making traffic untrackable or all headers confidential.','MajorfalseclaimQUICencryptsalmostwholeheader; routingCIDclear.')
md(n,'They protect against tampering by off-path parties, not against observers. The client\'s Initial must be padded to at least **1,200 bytes**,','Initial protection does not authenticate the peer against a party able to derive those public keys; TLS authenticates the handshake. A client must expand the **UDP payload** of each datagram carrying Initial packets to at least1,200bytes, by padding or coalescing packets,','PublicInitialkeysnotgeneraloffpathauthguarantee;1200appliesUDPdatagramnotindividualpacket.')
md(n,'Together these stop QUIC being used as a large DDoS amplifier with spoofed source addresses.','This bounds pre-validation amplification; it does not eliminate denial-of-service attacks or all reflection.','AmplificationlimitnotDDoSimmunity.')
md(n,'def varint(data):\n    length = 1 << (data[0] >> 6)','def varint(data):\n    if not data:\n        raise ValueError("empty varint")\n    length = 1 << (data[0] >> 6)\n    if len(data) < length:\n        raise ValueError("truncated varint")','Olddecoder silentlyacceptedtruncatedvarints.')
md(n,'QPACK sends table updates on separate streams so that a lost packet on one request stream does not block decoding the others.','QPACK sends table updates on dedicated streams and declares dependencies. Header blocks that reference unavailable dynamic entries can still block; encoders can avoid such references or bound the number of blocked streams.','QPACKreducesbutdoesnoteliminatecompressionHOL.')
md(n,'That ambiguity corrupts RTT estimates.','TCP avoids ambiguous samples using rules such as Karn’s algorithm; timestamps can also disambiguate samples.','TCPdoesnotblindlyusecorruptedRTTs.')
md(n,'QUIC packet numbers **only ever increase** and are never reused. Lost data is resent in a *new* packet with a new number; what is retransmitted is frames, not packets. ACK frames list ranges of received packet numbers and include the receiver\'s ACK delay, so RTT samples are clean.','QUIC packet numbers increase and are never reused **within each number space**. Required lost information is carried in new packets; it need not preserve old frame boundaries, and not every frame type is retransmitted. ACK ranges identify transmissions, avoiding TCP’s retransmission ambiguity. ACK-delay correction follows RFC9002 rules; reported delay and scheduling can still affect estimates.','Retransmitsinformationnotallframesidentically; no perfectlycleanRTTguarantee.')
a='A TCP connection is identified by the 4-tuple (source IP, source port, destination IP, destination port). If any changes, the connection is dead. Walk out of Wi-Fi range onto cellular and every TCP connection breaks. Even a NAT rebinding a port mid-session (common after an idle period) kills it.'
b='Ordinary TCP identifies an established connection by its endpoint addresses and ports. A changed externally visible tuple cannot simply continue that connection without additional mechanisms. A brief outage can recover if the tuple and state survive; VPNs, stable addressing or Multipath TCP can change handover behaviour. NAT rebinding can disrupt ordinary TCP, but neither every handover nor every idle period necessarily changes that tuple.'
md(n,a,b,'AvoidfalseallTCPconnectionsdieonwifi/cellular includingMPTCP/tunnels; timeout/rebindnotuniversal.')
md(n,'Each side hands the other several IDs in NEW_CONNECTION_ID frames ahead of time. On a new path the client switches to a **fresh** ID (B), so an observer cannot link the Wi-Fi and 5G traffic as the same connection.','When nonzero connection IDs are used, endpoints can issue spare IDs with NEW_CONNECTION_ID. On a deliberate new local-address path, a fresh peer-issued ID avoids linking paths by reusing that ID. It does not prevent correlation by timing, packet sizes or an observer that sees both paths.','FreshCIDreduceslinkability nottotalprivacy; zeroCIDexception.')
md(n,'That proves the client really is at the new address, rather than an attacker spoofing it to direct a flood at a victim.','A matching protected response validates reachability on that path. It does not establish a user’s physical location or defeat an attacker that can relay traffic.','Pathvalidationreachability notidentityorlocationproof.')
md(n,'so the sender restarts its congestion controller and RTT estimate.','so validation normally triggers resetting congestion and RTT state. RFC9000 permits retaining state for a port-only change and allows a cautious exception when the same path is known to remain in use.','Requiredexceptions toallmigrationcongestionreset.')
md(n,'A server can advertise a `preferred_address` once, and can disable migration with the `disable_active_migration` transport parameter.','A server can advertise preferred_address during the handshake. disable_active_migration restricts deliberate migration to the specified server address; it does not prohibit NAT rebinding or a permitted move to the server’s preferred address.','Parameterdoesnotdisableallmigration.')
md(n,'a migrated packet lands on a different server, which has no state for it. QUIC-aware load balancers route on the connection ID instead, and servers encode a routing hint inside the IDs they issue.','a changed tuple may select a different server without connection state; collisions or shared state can produce other outcomes. A coordinated CID-aware design can maintain routing across issued IDs, using a mapping or an encoded routing hint. It must also handle Initial routing and ID lifecycle.','Hashdifferentinputnotguaranteeddifferentserver; routinghintsnotuniversaldesign.')
md(n,'A browser cannot know in advance that a server speaks HTTP/3 over UDP. Usually it connects over TCP first and learns from an','A client can learn HTTP/3 support from configuration, cached knowledge, DNS or an','ContradictionDNSknowledgebeforeconnection; removeunsupportedusualbehavior.')
md(n,'then uses QUIC next time.','and may then open a QUIC connection.','Doesnotrequirewaitingnextvisit.')
md(n,'Browsers race QUIC against TCP, because some networks block UDP 443.','Clients can race or fall back to TCP when QUIC is unavailable; policy varies and UDP blocking is one possible cause.','Noallbrowsersguaranteedracing.')
md(n,'QUIC encrypts almost the whole header, uses 1/2/4/8-byte varints, and never reuses packet numbers, which makes loss recovery unambiguous.','QUIC encrypts payloads and protects selected header bits while leaving connection IDs visible. Varints use1/2/4/8bytes; packet numbers are unique within their number space.','Summarycorrectheaderprotectionandnumber-space.')
md(n,'QUIC connections are identified by connection IDs, so they survive address changes; path validation and fresh IDs keep migration safe and private.','With suitable connection IDs, routing and endpoint support, QUIC can survive address changes. Path validation and fresh IDs address specific reachability and linkability risks without guaranteeing availability or anonymity.','Migrationnotuniversalsurvivalprivacyguarantee.')
